  "pride_metadata": {
    "project": {
      "accession": "PXD061722",
      "title": "Quantitative proteomic and phosphoproteomic profiling of Sh3rf3-deficient ASD mouse model via 6Plex-TMT and phosphoproteomic",
      "additionalAttributes": [],
      "projectDescription": "This study aims to systematically investigate the molecular mechanisms underlying autism spectrum disorder (ASD) by analyzing the proteome and phosphoproteome of a Sh3rf3-deficient mouse model. SH3RF3 (SH3 domain-containing RING finger protein 3) is implicated in synaptic regulation pathways, yet its role in ASD remains poorly understood. Through integrated quantitative proteomics and phosphoproteomics, we seek to:Identify dysregulated proteins and phosphorylation events in Sh3rf3 knockout mice.Uncover signaling pathways and post-translational modifications (PTMs) associated with ASD pathophysiology.Provide a resource for understanding synaptic protein networks regulated by SH3RF3.",
      "sampleProcessingProtocol": "The mouse prefrontal cortex were digested with cell lysis (0.1M Tris-HCl pH 8.5 containing 4% sodium deoxycholate, Sigma D6750), and then sonicated (100 W, 5 seconds, 3 pulses). Lysates were centrifuged (13,000 \u00d7 g, 15 min, 4\u00b0C), and supernatants were boiled in SDS loading buffer (100\u00b0C, 5 min). The protein concentrations were detected using a BCA protein assay kit (Thermo Scientific, Rockford, IL). Protein digestion was performed using the filter-aided proteome preparation (FASP) method (2). Briefly, protein was reduced by 100 mM DTT, and the buffer was exchange by UA (8M urea, 100mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.5) using the Microcon YM-30 centrifugal filter units (EMD Millipore Corporation, Billerica, MA). After alkylated by 55 mM iodoacetamide (IAA, Sigma-Aldrich, Saint Louis, MO), a buffer containing 0.1 M triethylammonium bicarbonate (TEAB, Sigma-Aldrich, Saint Louis, MO) was used to replace the denaturing buffer of the sample. Proteins were then digested with trypsin (1:50 (w: w)) at 37 \u00b0C overnight, and the resultant tryptic peptides were labeled with acetonitrile-dissolved TMT reagents (Thermo Scientific, Rockford, IL) , and equal amount of labeled samples were mixed together before prefractionation with reversed phase (RP)-high performance liquid chromatography (HPLC).For TMT labeling,Protein digestion was performed using the filter-aided proteome preparation (FASP) method (2). Briefly, protein was reduced by 100 mM DTT, and the buffer was exchange by UA (8M urea, 100mM Tris-HCl, pH 8.5) using the Microcon YM-30 centrifugal filter units (EMD Millipore Corporation, Billerica, MA). After alkylated by 55 mM iodoacetamide (IAA, Sigma-Aldrich, Saint Louis, MO), a buffer containing 0.1 M triethylammonium bicarbonate (TEAB, Sigma-Aldrich, Saint Louis, MO) was used to replace the denaturing buffer of the sample. Proteins were then digested with trypsin (1:50 (w: w)) at 37 \u00b0C overnight, and the resultant tryptic peptides were labeled with acetonitrile-dissolved TMT reagents (Thermo Scientific, Rockford, IL) , and equal amount of labeled sampl",
      "dataProcessingProtocol": "For 6Plex-TMT,the database search was performed for all raw MS files using the software MaxQuant (version 2.3.1.0). The Mus musculus sequence database downloaded from Uniprot was applied to searching the data. The parameters used for the database search were set up as follows: The type of search: MS3; The type of isobaric labels:6-plex TMT; The minimum score for unmodified peptides: 15; The minimum score for modified peptides: 40; The variable modifications include N-terminal acetylation and methionine oxidation; The fix modification includes Cysteine carbamidomethylation. Default values were used for all other parameters.For phosphoproteomics,the database search was performed for all raw MS files using the software MaxQuant (version 2.4). The Mus musculus sequence database downloaded from Uniprot was applied to searching the data. The parameters used for the database search were set up as follows: The minimum score for unmodified peptides: 15; The minimum score for modified peptides: 40; The variable modifications include phosphorylation on STY, N-terminal acetylation and methionine oxidation; The fix modification includes Cysteine carbamidomethylation. The false discovery rates (FDR) for both peptide and protein identifications were set to 0.01. Default values were used for all other parameters.",
